```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: Agent serving systems that re-ingest tool results (e.g., JSON envelopes from database queries or API calls) where the same tool returns an identical schema with different value payloads, and the deployment currently uses vLLM APC/RadixAttention which halts reuse at the first differing token.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only. L0 CPU-only modeling shows position-aware reuse beyond APC of only 0.051–0.063 (5–6% of total tokens), below the pre-registered 10% HELD threshold (packet: "honest structural win over APC (c-a) = ONLY ~5-6%, below the pre-registered 10% HELD threshold"). RoPE tax (b-c) = 0.24–0.32 dominates the 0.42–0.44 token-level ceiling (packet: "RoPE tax (b-c) = 0.24-0.32 (dominant)"). Diag_where.csv confirms 85% of schema tokens live after the first variable value and only 14.9% (3448/23199) are position-reusable; the residual is coincidental same-length alignment, not a reliable production signal (packet: "Schema tokens AFTER the first variable value: only 14.9% reusable (3448/23199) — and 85% of all schema tokens live there"). In a production regime with paged kernels and APC already capturing the 100% position-stable pre-value prefix (3971/3971), the marginal 5–6% does not yield meaningful prefill FLOP or latency savings at scale.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] Runtime schema parsing to separate fixed envelope tokens from variable value spans adds complexity and maintenance burden for each tool; [2] 5–6% token reuse is insufficient ROI versus engineering cost; [3] Prior art already saturates the space: Prompt Cache (Gim et al.) implements position-independent modular segment reuse achieving the 42–44% ceiling, and CacheBlend implements the R(Δ) re-anchor to recover RoPE tax (packet: "Prompt Cache (Gim et al.) ALREADY does position-INDEPENDENT modular segment reuse = the mechanism that realizes the 42-44% ceiling. CacheBlend = the re-anchor R(delta) that recovers the tax. So WITH re-anchor the scheme collapses to CacheBlend; WITHOUT it's a weaker (5-6%) special case of Prompt Cache. No novelty gap."); [4] RoPE position-dependence is fundamental: without re-anchor, post-value schema KV is not reusable; with re-anchor, the design is not distinct from CacheBlend; [5] L0 uses a deterministic word/punct tokenizer, not real BPE, and CPU-only serial execution; production GPU kernels and tokenizer artifacts will likely erode the 5–6% further.
RATIONALE: The packet's L0 verdict is NEGATIVE with an honest negative = WIN (packet: "VERDICT: NEGATIVE (honest negative = WIN)"). Part (A) holds—the opportunity exists (APC misses 30–38% of schema)—but Part (B) fails because RoPE kills position stability (packet: "Part (A) HOLDS... Part (B) FAILS: RoPE position-dependence makes that structure NOT reusable"). The actionable guidance is clean: do not build template-structured KV reuse without re-anchoring; use Prompt Cache for position-independent reuse or CacheBlend for re-anchored reuse. The 5–6% win is a modeling artifact of coincidental length alignment, not a production-ready optimization.
```
